{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1478894-contested-vs-resisted","id":1478894,"title":"Contested vs Resisted","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-04-15T02:23:39-07:00","id":1478894,"title":"Contested vs Resisted","content":"Core says, as a generic rule, you should use Resisted whenever numbers of successes matter and Contested when not.<br /> I personally prefer to adopt a different approach which I'd like to hear your thoughts about.<br /> I usually use resisted whenever the other party is not directly attempting to resist or taking any action which is going against yours and Contested whenever a direct action or attempt is taken.<br /> I'd try to make some example to clarify:<br /> <br /> hiding from/shadowing a target:<ul><li>Target is unaware of being so or not suspicious: Resisted wits+Stealth - wits</li> <li>Target is aware you are hiding somewhere or he could be stalked and is actively looking around to spot anything: contested wits+ stealth vs wits+composure</li> </ul>trying to fast talking someone:<ul><li>target has no reasons to believe the opposite or to doubt about you: resisted: manip+subterfuge - composure</li> <li>target is actively trying to understand whether he can trust you or not or has reason to doubt you (e.g.: interrogation): contested manip+subterfuge vs wits+composure or composure+empatia</li> </ul>etc. In fact, also in combat you are resisting with defense when you are not taking an action to counter an incoming attack, you are only using your natural elusiveness. While you are contesting rolling defensex2 when you are using your whole action to elude an attack.<br /> <br /> The reason which took me to this approach is that, using resisted rolls as per core, is statistically to easy to succeed also against NPC which shouldn't be too easy to convince. Having a pool of 3 dice after subtracting 5 dice for the composure of an elder, is still statistically &quot;often&quot; a success. While, contesting an 8 dicepool against a10 dice pool, give an edge to the elder (just an example).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-15T04:26:37-07:00","id":1478898,"title":"","content":"It's a very valid take on it. Switching between resisted and contested based on whether it should be relatively easy or not is a fast and simple way to affect difficulty without having to determine a specific numerical modifier based on &quot;receptiveness&quot;.<br /> It also connects the difficulty to whether the opponent resists passively or actively (just like Defense/Dodge already does), which makes the type of roll much more intuitive and deepens the connection between the physical roll of the dice with the in-game action (which is why it's used by some other RPGs, most famously D&amp;D 5e).<br /> <br /> The one downside is that this system extremely heavily favours successful rolls (provided the character has any sort of competence in the field) and using only resistance might not have much of an effect on the statistics of getting a success. Note that Defense is made out of 2 traits, and is still expected to be overcome regularly, while other resisted rolls only use a single resistance trait.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1478894-contested-vs-resisted#post1478894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The reason which took me to this approach is that, using resisted rolls as per core, is statistically to easy to succeed also against NPC which shouldn't be too easy to convince. Having a pool of 3 dice after subtracting 5 dice for the composure of an elder, is still statistically &amp;quot;often&amp;quot; a success. While, contesting an 8 dicepool against a10 dice pool, give an edge to the elder (just an example).</div> </div> </div> </div>Note that this type of roll should already be contested. The example dice pools in the core books often directly break the rule about when rolls should be contested or resisted. In general, every single roll against another character is expected to be contested because success is supposed to be binary: You succeed or you don't.<br /> Combat is obviously a huge exception to this rule, but combat breaks a bunch of rules: I already mentioned Defense being two traits, but there's also Dodge being the same (composite) trait twice instead of two different traits, and Dodge rolls are possibly the only example of a contested roll where successes are counted (which is why the Dodge rule has to specify that your successes cancel out the opponent's successes).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T10:16:31-07:00","id":1479018,"title":"","content":"If you follow the rules instead of the examples, your statistic is off in account of the number of successes. As a rule of thumb, an action to which the number of successes matters naturally needs an edge because <i>getting one isn't enough</i>.<br /> <br /> The contexts in which such actions happen are also frequently heavier with dice rolls, and here's the big problem with Contested Action in general, it doubles the rolls and triples the math for what is still a single action.<br /> <br /> Contested is good when an action is dramatic enough to be worth the extra time with the dice. You get the sense that both parties are important actors in the action and their contest will define who gets to have things their way, one wins, the other loses, there's no middle ground.<br /> <br /> Outside of that you really risk bogging down the game with extra rolls and calculations for a negligible perceived gain in statistical validity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}